Douglas, Brendan,

Following up on the questions from yesterday's Investment Committee.

1. TSMC advanced packaging. The constraint is CoWoS-class packaging, not lead=
ing-edge wafers. Our estimate is that TSMC keeps adding packaging capacity th=
rough 2026, but not fast enough to close the gap with accelerator demand thro=
ugh 2027. Until it does, packaging rather than wafer supply sets how many hig=
h-end GPUs reach the market.

2. GPU capacity. Our channel checks say supply is still being allocated rathe=
r than filled on demand, with long lead times on the newest Nvidia platforms,=
 while hyperscaler capex budgets are still rising. The other limits we track =
are high-bandwidth memory supply and power availability at new data centers. =
We do not expect any of these to ease meaningfully in the next few quarters.

3. Concentration. Elena Vasquez's point is fair - Nvidia sits in both the AI =
Infrastructure Fund and the Global Technology Fund, where Olivia Grant puts i=
t at about 8%. Any sizing proposal has to address that overlap directly.

On sizing: the AI Infrastructure Fund is 5% of the Growth model and 3% of the=
 Balanced model. My suggestion is to put sizing on the January 15 agenda and =
decide then whether that is still enough given the outlook. The detailed supp=
ly-demand work is in the November report Arjun Mehta and I published, "GPU De=
mand Outlook 2026-2027: Capacity Remains Tight".

